A||||10||Buster||wheels leaking||||1||
Z||000000||Buster||07-12-2000||08:35 PM||||I recently purchased an 88 Formula V6/auto. It has the 6x15 fronts & 7x15 rears, both black diamonds. Besides the 205's / 215's being on the wrong rims, they all leak alot. As I tear them down to match tires to wheels is there anything I should do to help the leak down. They will drop from 30psi to 10psi in less than a week. I will check fo puncture. All 4 are Michelins.        Thanks   Buster||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-12-2000||09:03 PM||||Hard to diagnose leaky tires.  Once you get the right size tire on the right rim, you'd be just resealing the bead which may cure the problem.  Valve stems have been known to leak.  A cracked rim.  If all the tires are leaking (strange occurance)may just be defective tires. I've only had one tire at a time have a leak, once it was a nail, that I found when I got new tires.  The other on a rear 88 black lace rim.  Fix a flat wouldn't keep air in it.  I got new rims and tires.  The old rims and tires went to storage, but that tire now has held the air for two years.  Go figure, the weight of the car on the rim was forcing the air out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||07-12-2000||09:51 PM||||This might sound crazy but was yakking with my tire guy and he had some tires once that wouldn't hold air and he couldn't find the leaks. He put them in the water tank with 10 psi in them -no leaks then 60 psi -no leaks. Put them on the car -leaks. Turned out it was the valve caps, they had a little flash or something inside and was just enough push the plunger down. I know this doesn't help you but it's still an interesting story.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||07-13-2000||07:44 AM||||I'd say hit up the stems, I've found those to be a rather weak link in the tire system. Expecially when you consider that little piece of rubber has been getting mucked on for the last 12+ years all the while 30psi are pushing out on it. And they're cheap too!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||07-13-2000||12:43 PM||||If you have the pound on wheel weights, take them off and see if that makes a difference.  I've had wheels leak there.  Have you had your wheels checked for out of round?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000005||lennyb4||07-13-2000||01:03 PM||||I had the same problem with three of my tires i had put on around 4 months ago. If the person putting on the new tires does not properly clean the inner rim area then you will get slow leaks out of the bead. I took them back up had them pop the tires off and wallaa....The guy never cleaned off the old sealent and crap..They cleaned them up w/a wire brush (air powered of course) put the tires back on w/sealent and havent leaked at all since..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallPegasus.gif">  <BR>87 Coupe... Got rice???||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||07-13-2000||02:39 PM||||<BR>I second lennyb4.<P>Beads can cause major problems if they are not properly cleaned.  And being that the alloy wheels are 12 years old, chances are that some oxidation has occured between the rim and the tire bead which is causing air loss.<P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 07-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-13-2000||04:14 PM||||The simple thing to always check is that vavle cores are tight in the stems. I've seen new stems come with loose cores more than a few times.<P>Yeah bead leaks will do this for sure. annoying as hell to. At the least you need to knock down all the wheels. Check all the valve stem holes too while it's apart.<P>One thing to consider is taking the rubber off. strip the inner surface of the wheels and recoat the whole thing including the bead area. That will solve both a bead problem and wheel porosity problem in one pass. Do this with the valve stems out so the holes get coated too. Crap in the stem holes will cause this just the same as a bead. You want the coating spray that's made for this job, not paint. I think Eastwood has it if you can't find it at pepboys or something. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||PAUL88GT||07-13-2000||04:58 PM||||One of my lace wheels was leaking slowly. The guy at the tire shop removed the tire cleaned the back inside of the rim and coated the tire/wheel interface area with what looked to be wheel bearing grease. He charged me $5.00 and it hasn't leaked since.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Big O||07-14-2000||01:01 AM||||Bead sealer is what it's called.  I agree that you must have a clean seat for the bead.  Make sure the beads aren't torn when the tires are installed.  This can also result in leaks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-14-2000||02:16 PM||||ive had a few leak at bead, i sprayed some WD40 on tire at bead, had 1 i had to break down and clean too. All it takes to leak is a little bit of dirt or rust.||||reg||1
